    "summary": "A group of Somali religious scholars has publicly supported Saudi Arabia's military intervention against the Houthi movement in Yemen, condemning the group's actions.",
    "content": "A number of Somali religious scholars have expressed strong support for the Saudi-led military campaign against the Houthi movement in Yemen. In a statement released this week, the scholars condemned the Houthis' actions and endorsed the Saudi intervention, according to Horseed Media.\n\nThe conflict in Yemen has been ongoing since 2014, when the Houthis took control of the capital, Sanaa. In March 2015, Saudi Arabia launched a military campaign to restore the internationally recognized Yemeni government. The Houthis are backed by Iran, while Saudi Arabia leads a coalition of Arab states.\n\nSomali scholars who signed the statement argued that the Houthi movement poses a threat to regional stability and to the security of the Muslim world. They called on Muslims to support the Saudi-led coalition and to oppose the Houthis, whom they accuse of destabilizing Yemen and harming its people.\n\nThis development is significant for the Somali diaspora, which has strong ties to both Yemen and Saudi Arabia. Yemen hosts a large Somali refugee population, and many Somalis have family members there. Additionally, Saudi Arabia is a major destination for Somali labor migrants and a key source of remittances. The stance of Somali scholars could influence public opinion within the diaspora regarding the Yemen conflict.\n\nThe Somali government has officially maintained a neutral position on the Yemen war, but individual religious leaders and groups have occasionally taken sides. The scholars' support for the Saudi campaign reflects broader geopolitical and sectarian divisions in the region.\n\nRead the original report from Horseed Media here: https://horseedmedia.net/culimo-soomaali-ah-oo-taageeray-duullaanka-sucuudigu-ku-qaaday-xuutiyiinta-yemen/425876/",
