    "original_title": "ONLF Oo Xaqiijisay Ku Biirista Isbahaysiga Itoobiya, Diiddayna Bayaan Ka Soo Horjeeday",
    "slug": "onlf-confirms-membership-in-new-ethiopian-opposition-coalition",
    "summary": "The Ogaden National Liberation Front (ONLF) has confirmed its participation in a newly formed coalition of seven Ethiopian armed groups seeking to remove Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed from power.",
    "content": "The Ogaden National Liberation Front (ONLF) has confirmed that it is a member of a new coalition comprising seven Ethiopian armed organizations. The coalition, named the Ethiopian People's Armed Forces for Salvation, was announced on September 20. In a statement released on September 21, the ONLF denied an earlier declaration that had been circulated in its name, which had claimed it was not part of the alliance.\n\nThe coalition includes groups from Ethiopia's Tigray, Amhara, Oromia, Afar, Somali, Benishangul-Gumuz, and Gumuz regions. These factions have stated their shared objective is to remove Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed's government. The formation of this alliance marks a significant escalation in opposition to Abiy's administration, which has faced mounting criticism over its handling of regional conflicts and governance.\n\nThe ONLF, which has historically represented ethnic Somali interests in Ethiopia's Ogaden region, has been engaged in a long-running insurgency against the Ethiopian government. In recent years, the group has shifted toward political engagement, but its inclusion in this new armed coalition signals a potential return to militant strategies. The denial of the earlier statement suggests internal disagreements or confusion over the coalition's composition.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development is particularly significant. Many diaspora members have family ties to the Ogaden region and have closely followed the ONLF's activities. The coalition's formation could impact the political and security landscape in the Horn of Africa, potentially affecting diaspora communities in Somalia, Kenya, and beyond. It also raises questions about the future of Ethiopian-Somali relations and the stability of the region.\n\nThe announcement comes amid ongoing tensions in Ethiopia, where multiple armed groups are challenging federal authority. The coalition's ability to coordinate actions across diverse regions remains uncertain, but its formation underscores the growing opposition to Abiy's leadership. The ONLF's confirmation of membership may also strain its relations with the Ethiopian government, which has previously engaged in peace talks with the group.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/23/onlf-oo-xaqiijisay-ku-biirista-isbahaysiga-itoobiya-diiddayna-bayaan-ka-soo-horjeeday/",
