    "content": "The United Nations Office for the Coordination of Humanitarian Affairs (OCHA) has issued a warning that upcoming El Niño rains could affect up to 3.4 million people in Somalia. The agency said the risk of flooding is expected to rise between October and December, with heavy rains likely to impact 16 of the country's 18 regions.\n\nAccording to OCHA, the regions of Hiraan, Middle Juba, and Lower Juba are of particular concern. The expected floods could be similar to or more severe than those experienced in previous flood seasons. The warning follows patterns of extreme weather that have repeatedly hit Somalia in recent years.\n\nSomalia has faced recurring droughts and floods in recent years, both of which have displaced hundreds of thousands of people and strained humanitarian resources. El Niño, a climate pattern that warms Pacific Ocean waters, often leads to above-average rainfall in the Horn of Africa. OCHA's warning is part of its ongoing monitoring of humanitarian risks in the country.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the warning signals a potential new humanitarian crisis in a homeland still recovering from past climate shocks. Many diaspora members have family members in the affected regions and often mobilize to send remittances or organize relief efforts when disasters strike. The scale of the projected flooding could increase demands for diaspora support.\n\nOCHA and its partners are likely to scale up preparedness and response activities in the coming weeks. The agency has not yet specified the exact number of people who might be displaced or the extent of damage expected, but the warning serves as an early alert for humanitarian planning.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/23/qaramada-midoobay-oo-ka-digtay-in-roobabka-el-nino-ay-saameyn-ku-yeelan-karaan-ilaa-3-4-milyan-oo-qof-oo-soomaali-ah/",
